the one exception, and what it is and is not
      1  //! Packed write, the naive parallel encoder a caller would produce without
      2  //! knowing about the packed representation's own period, run against
      3  //! `bitpack-write-contend-race`'s deliberately misaligned sizes.
      4  //!
      5  //! This is the same `kern_packed_plain` function `bitpack-write-aligned`
      6  //! runs; only the `(N, T)` pair differs, which is the whole point: the
      7  //! kernel is not the defect, the split is. Included as a demonstration arm,
      8  //! not as a candidate. `bitpack-write-contend-shared`'s own stress tests
      9  //! (`stress.rs`) already measured this exact kernel corrupting 16 to 19
     10  //! percent of concurrent passes at a misaligned split on this host; this
     11  //! variant exists to show what that costs (or does not cost) against the
     12  //! guarded fix, not to compete for the fastest time. The harness's own
     13  //! cross-arm validation may or may not catch an individual run's corruption
     14  //! (a race is not guaranteed to fire on every pass), which is itself part of
     15  //! the finding: a defect that is not always caught is worse than one that
     16  //! fails loudly every time.

  So one arm may compute a different answer, on purpose, and its own
  header calls it a demonstration arm rather than a candidate. That is
  a CORRECTNESS difference from a data race, not an accuracy difference
  from quantisation, and its magnitude is measured in the crate's stress
  test as a corruption RATE over trials rather than as a bench column.
  A rate of wrong answers is exactly the shape of an accuracy
  coordinate, and it sits in a test rather than in the CSV, so no
  weighting can read it.

what follows
  A family whose arms are required to agree cannot measure an accuracy
  difference between them, because there is none to measure. That is
  correct bench design and it is the right requirement: without it the
  fast arm is fast because it is doing less.

  It does mean that no committed bench family carries a column for how
  wrong an arm is, nor one for how far an arm departs from a reference
  semantics. So of the four intents op has stated, two have coordinates
  in the committed corpus (time and footprint) and two do not. A
  weighting cannot distinguish strategies along a coordinate nobody has
  varied, so on this corpus the accuracy-first and the
  imitate-the-native-primitive intents have no expression at all.

  This is a gap in the corpus rather than in the design, and it is the
  kind op named as inside the panel's scope at `95`: a harness question
  that blocks pricing. What is missing is a family whose arms compute
  DIFFERENT answers on purpose, with the difference measured against an
  exact reference and reported as a column. `35`'s law probes measure
  exactly that quantity and are not bench arms; the write-unsound arm
  has exactly that property and reports no such column; nothing joins
  the two.

and the same question asked of the arms themselves
  In the carrier family used by p1 and p6, every arm decodes the same
  13-bit column and the crate asserts the decode against an independent
  ground truth, so the five or six arms differ in time and footprint and
  in nothing else. Any weighting over that family is a weighting over
  two coordinates, whatever the strategy claims to weigh.

